.TH "PERFORMANCEPAINTTIMING" "3JS" "August 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformancePaintTiming \- PerformancePaintTiming
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]PerformancePaintTiming\f[B]\f[R] interface provides
timing information about \(lqpaint\(rq (also called \(lqrender\(rq)
operations during web page construction.
\(lqPaint\(rq refers to conversion of the render tree to on\-screen
pixels.
.PP
There are two key paint moments this API provides:
.IP \(bu 2
First Paint (FP): Time when anything is rendered.
Note that the marking of the first paint is optional, not all user
agents report it.
.IP \(bu 2
First Contentful Paint (FCP): Time when the first contentful paint \(em
the first bit of DOM text or image content is rendered.
.PP
A third key paint moment is provided by the
\f[CR]LargestContentfulPaint\f[R] API:
.IP \(bu 2
Largest Contentful Paint (LCP): Render time of the largest image or text
block visible within the viewport, recorded from when the page first
begins to load.
.PP
The data this API provides helps you minimize the time that users have
to wait before they can see the site\(cqs content start to appear.
Decreasing the time until these key paint moments make sites feel more
responsive, performant, and engaging for your users.
.PP
Like other Performance APIs, this API extends
\f[CR]PerformanceEntry\f[R].
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
This interface directly defines the following properties:
.TP
\f[B]PerformancePaintTiming.paintTime\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]timestamp\f[R] when the rendering phase ended and the
paint phase started.
.TP
\f[B]PerformancePaintTiming.presentationTime\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]timestamp\f[R] when the painted pixels were actually
drawn on the screen.
.PP
It also extends the following \f[CR]PerformanceEntry\f[R] properties,
qualifying and constraining them as described:
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.entryType\f[R]
Returns \f[CR]\(dqpaint\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.name\f[R]
Returns either \f[CR]\(dqfirst\-paint\(dq\f[R] or
\f[CR]\(dqfirst\-contentful\-paint\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.startTime\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]timestamp\f[R] when the paint occurred.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.duration\f[R]
Returns 0.
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
.TP
\f[B]PerformancePaintTiming.toJSON()\f[R]
Overrides the \f[CR]PerformanceEntry.toJSON()\f[R] method to return a
JSON representation of the \f[CR]PerformancePaintTiming\f[R] object.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Getting basic paint timings
Example using a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R], which notifies of new
\f[CR]paint\f[R] performance entries as they are recorded in the
browser\(cqs performance timeline.
Use the \f[CR]buffered\f[R] option to access entries from before the
observer creation.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  list.getEntries().forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    console.log(
      \(gaThe time to ${entry.name} was ${entry.startTime} milliseconds.\(ga,
    );
    \f[I]// Logs \(dqThe time to first\-paint was 386.7999999523163 milliseconds.\(dq\f[R]
    \f[I]// Logs \(dqThe time to first\-contentful\-paint was 400.6999999284744 milliseconds.\(dq\f[R]
  });
});

observer.observe({ type: \(dqpaint\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
.EE
.PP
Example using \f[CR]Performance.getEntriesByType()\f[R], which only
shows \f[CR]paint\f[R] performance entries present in the browser\(cqs
performance timeline at the time you call this method:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] entries = performance.getEntriesByType(\(dqpaint\(dq);
entries.forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(gaThe time to ${entry.name} was ${entry.startTime} milliseconds.\(ga);
  \f[I]// Logs \(dqThe time to first\-paint was 386.7999999523163 milliseconds.\(dq\f[R]
  \f[I]// Logs \(dqThe time to first\-contentful\-paint was 400.6999999284744 milliseconds.\(dq\f[R]
});
.EE
.SS Getting separate paint and presentation timings
The \f[CR]paintTime\f[R] and \f[CR]presentationTime\f[R] properties
enable you to retrieve specific timings for the paint phase starting and
the painted pixels being drawn on the screen.
The \f[CR]paintTime\f[R] is broadly interoperable, whereas the
\f[CR]presentationTime\f[R] is implementation\-dependent.
.PP
This example builds on the earlier
\f[CR]Performance.getEntriesByType()\f[R] example, showing how to check
for \f[CR]paintTime\f[R] and \f[CR]presentationTime\f[R] support and
retrieve those values if they are available.
In non\-supporting browsers, the code retrieves the \f[CR]loadTime\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] entries = performance.getEntriesByType(\(dqpaint\(dq);
entries.forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (entry.presentationTime) {
    console.log(
      \(dqpaintTime:\(dq,
      entry.paintTime,
      \(dqpresentationTime:\(dq,
      entry.presentationTime,
    );
  } \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (entry.paintTime) {
    console.log(\(dqpaintTime:\(dq, entry.paintTime);
  } \f[B]else\f[R] {
    console.log(\(dqloadTime\(dq, entry.loadTime);
  }
});
.EE
.SS See also
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]LargestContentfulPaint\f[R]
